A||||2||billfredmike24||Subwoofer question??||||1||
Z||000000||billfredmike24||11-20-2001||04:45 PM||||I have a 10" kenwood subwoofer that had a screwdriver dropped on the cone and is now cracked from the center to the the outside about 4".  It cracked cleanly and you can't tell it's cracked unless yo push on it.  Is there any way to have this fixed, if so, how? or is it garbage?<BR>TIA Mike<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigt_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||11-20-2001||06:00 PM||||What you can try is this (Disclaimer: I just came up with this so I have never actually tried this myself. No guarantees it will actually work):<P>You need two things: thin glue that should be applied with a brush, and medical tape (this is thin and white and has a paper texture).<P>Apply the glue over the top of the crack, then apply the tape over the glue. Let it dry.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||11-20-2001||06:55 PM||||Well, I've heard on smaller speakers you can use fingernail polish, which I've tried with success.  I don't think this would work with a sub, though.<P>You could try a two part epoxy that gets hard.  It wouldn't take too much, and you could apply it on both sides.  Some of that stuff gets REALLY hard, if you get the right kind and mix it right.  I recommend sampling the epoxy before putting it on your sub, to see if it will be strong enough.  Once that stuff's on, there's no easy way to get it off.<P>Another idea is fiberglass resin, same concept (two part epoxy).<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
